[[:db/cas 17592186069389 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}e^{5x}\\cos xdx$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}e^{5x}\\cos xdx.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069390 :question/problem "$\\displaystyle \\int(-x^{2} - 10x + 4)/((x + 5)(x^{2} + x + 9))dx =$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int(-x^{2} - 10x + 4)/((x + 5)(x^{2} + x + 9))dx =\n$$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186069395 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{-7x - 15}{x^{2} + 6x + 9}dx$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{-7x - 15}{x^{2} + 6x + 9}dx.\n$$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186069396 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int(-5)/(x(x - 1))dx$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int(-5)/(x(x - 1))dx\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069397 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069397 :question/worked-solution "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+1$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)({x}^{2}+1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n2=A({x}^{2}+1)+(Bx+C)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=-1,$ and $C=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{-x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{-x-1}{{x}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+1| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+K\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The quadratic factor ${x}^{2}+1$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{Bx+C}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)({x}^{2}+1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n2=A({x}^{2}+1)+(Bx+C)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=1,B=-1,$ and $C=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{-x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{-x-1}{{x}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the substitution $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|{x}^{2}+1| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral, we use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right),\n$$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+K\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069398 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}e^{-x}\\sin xdx$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}e^{-x}\\sin xdx.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069399 :question/problem "$\\int 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069400 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{\\sqrt{3} - 3}\\frac{8}{x^{2} + 6x + 10}dx$." "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{\\sqrt{3} - 3}\\frac{8}{x^{2} + 6x + 10}dx.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069401 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z$ using integration by parts." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ using integration by parts."] [:db/cas 17592186069401 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{3}⁡z\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{1}{z}\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)\\left(\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I \\\\ 4I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡z}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡z){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡(e)-{ln}^{4}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the indefinite integral by $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{3}⁡z\\, & ⇒\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}=\\frac{1}{z}\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{3}⁡z(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)\\left(\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡z}{z}\\right)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3\\int \\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $I.$ We can ignore the constant of integration in this case, because it is insignificant for further evaluation of the definite integral.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z-3I \\\\ 4I & ={ln}^{4}⁡z \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{4}⁡z}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{{ln}^{3}⁡z}{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡z){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({ln}^{4}⁡(e)-{ln}^{4}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069415 :question/problem "Given that\n$(5x^{2} + x + 3)/(x(x^{2} + 1)) = \\frac{2x + B}{x^{2} + 1} + \\frac{3}{x}$,\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\displaystyle \\int(5x^{2} + x + 3)/(x(x^{2} + 1))dx$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle (5x^{2} + x + 3)/(x(x^{2} + 1)) = \\frac{2x + B}{x^{2} + 1} + \\frac{3}{x},\n$$\nwhere $B$ is a constant to be found, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int(5x^{2} + x + 3)/(x(x^{2} + 1))dx.\n$$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186069416 :question/problem "Given that\n$(4x^{2} - 3x + 5)/((x - 1)(x^{2} + 2)) = \\frac{Ax - 1}{x^{2} + 2} + \\frac{2}{x - 1}$,\nwhere $A$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\displaystyle \\int(4x^{2} - 3x + 5)/((x - 1)(x^{2} + 2))dx$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle (4x^{2} - 3x + 5)/((x - 1)(x^{2} + 2)) = \\frac{Ax - 1}{x^{2} + 2} + \\frac{2}{x - 1},\n$$\nwhere $A$ is a constant to be found, 