\\phantom{00}} \\pm  \\sqrt{ {4}^{2} - 4 \\cdot  4 \\cdot  1}}{2 \\cdot  4}}\n$$\nWhat number is missing from the formula, and what is the solution of the equation?"] [:db/cas 17592186069496 :question/problem "What is $\\frac{7x}{2y^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{4y}{x^{2}}$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{7x}{2y^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{4y}{x^{2}}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069497 :question/problem "What is $\\frac{5b}{3a^{3}} \\cdot \\frac{6a}{b^{2}}$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5b}{3a^{3}} \\cdot \\frac{6a}{b^{2}}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069507 :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069509 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{3}-x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{3}-x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069515 :question/problem "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x}?$" "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069516 :question/problem "Solve for $n$ where $\\frac{5n - 2}{2n} = \\frac{n + 7}{2n} + \\frac{1 - n}{2n}$." "Solve for $n$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5n - 2}{2n} = \\frac{n + 7}{2n} + \\frac{1 - n}{2n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069517 :question/problem "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{x + 3}{x} = \\frac{x - 4}{4x} + \\frac{1}{x}$?" "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x + 3}{x} = \\frac{x - 4}{4x} + \\frac{1}{x}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069519 :question/problem "Solve for $n$ where $\\frac{3n + 2}{n} = \\frac{n + 3}{n} + \\frac{n - 1}{n}$." "Solve for $n$ where $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3n + 2}{n} = \\frac{n + 3}{n} + \\frac{n - 1}{n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069520 :question/problem "Which value of $p$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2p + 5}{4p} = \\frac{p - 3}{p} - \\frac{p + 2}{8p}$?" "Which value of $p$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2p + 5}{4p} = \\frac{p - 3}{p} - \\frac{p + 2}{8p}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069521 :question/problem "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3}{x} - \\frac{x - 2}{x^{2}} = \\frac{x + 1}{x^{2}}$?" "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{x} - \\frac{x - 2}{x^{2}} = \\frac{x + 1}{x^{2}}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186069530 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{4x}{4x^{2} + 4x + 1}dx$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{4x}{4x^{2} + 4x + 1}dx.\n$$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186069531 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069974 :tutorial/content "The long division method allows us to divide a polynomial by another nonlinear polynomial.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-4x^3+4x^2+x+4}{4x^2-1}\n$$\n\nHere, the divisor is the quadratic polynomial $4x^2-1$. \n\nAgain, we follow a step-by-step process.\n\n**Step 1**\n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}{-4x^3}})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{red}{-4x^3}}{{\\color{blue}{4x^2}}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-x}}.$ We add this result to the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the $\\color{#8b008b}-x$ we just found by the full divisor ${\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}-x}({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1)=-4x^3+x.$ We place this underneath the dividend, being sure to match the terms. Notice that we include a zero coefficient for the $x^2$ term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $-4x^3+0x^2+x$ from $-4x^3+4x^2+x+4$ and get $4x^2+0x+4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+0x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**\n\nWe now repeat the process. The new dividend is ${\\color{red}{4x^2}}+0x+4.$\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}{4x^2}})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}{4x^2}}}{{\\color{blue}{4x^2}}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{1}}.$ We  add this result to the quotient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x+1 \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+0x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{1}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{1}}({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1)=4x^2-1.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x+1 \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+0x+4 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}+0x-1}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract $4x^2+0x-1$ from $4x^2+0x+4$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x+1 \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+0x+4 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}+0x-1}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're left with the constant $\\color{red}5$, which has a smaller degree than the divisor. So, the division is complete.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is ${\\color{blue}{-x+1}},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}.$\n\nOur complete result can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-4x^3+4x^2+x+4}{4x^2-1}={\\color{blue}{-x+1}} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{4x^2-1}\n$$" "The long division method allows us to divide a polynomial by another nonlinear polynomial.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-4x^3+4x^2+x+4}{4x^2-1}\n$$\n\nHere, the divisor is the quadratic polynomial $4x^2-1$. \n\nAgain, we follow a step-by-step process.\n\n**Step 1**\n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}{-4x^3}})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}).$ This gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{\\color{red}{-4x^3}}{{\\color{blue}{4x^2}}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-x}}.\n$$ We add this result to the quotient line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the $\\color{#8b008b}-x$ we just found by the full divisor ${\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}-x}({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1)=-4x^3+x.$ We place this underneath the dividend, being sure to match the terms. Notice that we include a zero coefficient for the $x^2$ term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $-4x^3+0x^2+x$ from $-4x^3+4x^2+x+4$ and get $4x^2+0x+4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+0x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**\n\nWe now repeat the process. The new dividend is ${\\color{red}{4x^2}}+0x+4.$\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}{4x^2}})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}).$ This gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}{4x^2}}}{{\\color{blue}{4x^2}}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{1}}.\n$$ We  add this result to the quotient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x+1 \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+0x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{1}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{1}}({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1)=4x^2-1.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x+1 \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+0x+4 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}+0x-1}_{―}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract $4x^2+0x-1$ from $4x^2+0x+4$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-x+1 \\\\  & 4{x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}x+4\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+0x+4 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}+0x-1}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're left with the constant $\\color{red}5$, which has a smaller degree than the divisor. So, the division is complete.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is ${\\color{blue}{-x+1}},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}.$\n\nOur complete result can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-4x^3+4x^2+x+4}{4x^2-1}={\\color{blue}{-x+1}} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{4x^2-1}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069991 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to find \n\n$$\n{\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\frac {\\sin x}{x} }.\n$$\n\nIf we try to plug in $x=0,$ we get the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ So what can we do? \n\nFortunately, we can find this limit using **L'Hopital's Rule**, which states that if $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x) = 0$ and $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}g(x) = 0,$ then \n\n$$\n\\\n\\lim_{x\\to a}  \\dfrac {f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{x\\to a}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)},\n$$\n\nprovided that $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable at $x=a,$ and that $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to a}  \\dfrac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists.\n\nSo, by applying L'Hopital's rule to our limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac {\\sin x}{x} &= \\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {[\\sin x]'}{[x]'}\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac {\\cos x}{1}\\\\[5pt]\n&=  \\cos 0\\\\[5pt]\n&= 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that we want to find \n\n$$\n{\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\frac {\\sin x}{x} }.\n$$\n\nIf we try to plug in $x=0,$ we get the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ So what can we do? \n\nFortunately, we can find this limit using **L'Hopital's Rule**, which states that if $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x) = 0\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}g(x) = 0,\n$$ then \n\n$$\n\\\n\\lim_{x\\to a}  \\dfrac {f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{x\\to a}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)},\n$$\n\nprovided that $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable at $x=a,$ and that $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to a}  \\dfrac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists.\n\nSo, by applying L'Hopital's rule to our limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac {\\sin x}{x} &= \\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {[\\sin x]'}{[x]'}\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac {\\cos x}{1}\\\\[5pt]\n&=  \\cos 0\\\\[5pt]\n&= 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186069992 :tutorial/content "We can also use L'Hopital's rule to deal with the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}$. In this case, L'Hopital's rule states that if $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x) = \\pm\\infty$ and $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}g(x) = \\pm\\infty,$ then \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}  \\dfrac {f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{x\\to a}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)},\n$$\n\nprovided that $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable at $x=a,$ and the limit $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to a}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists." "We can also use L'Hopital's rule to deal with the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}$. In this case, L'Hopital's rule states that if $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}f(x) = \\pm\\infty\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow a}g(x) = \\pm\\infty,\n$$ then \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a}  \\dfrac {f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{x\\to a}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)},\n$$\n\nprovided that $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable at $x=a,$ and the limit $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to a}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists."] [:db/cas 17592186069993 :tutorial/content "L'Hopital's rule  can be  extended to the case in which  the limit is taken as $x\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty.$ In this case, L'Hopital's rule  states that if either\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow  \\pm\\infty}f(x) = \\pm \\infty$ and $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty }g(x) = \\pm \\infty,$ or\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow  \\pm\\infty}f(x) = 0$ and $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty }g(x) = 0,$\n\nthen \n\n$$\n\\\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm\\infty}  \\dfrac {f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{x\\to \\pm\\infty}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)},\n$$\n\nprovided that $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable for all sufficiently large $x,$ and the limit $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to \\pm\\infty}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists." "L'Hopital's rule  can be  extended to the case in which  the limit is taken as $x\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty.$ In this case, L'Hopital's rule  states that if either\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow  \\pm\\infty}f(x) = \\pm \\infty\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty }g(x) = \\pm \\infty,\n$$ or\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow  \\pm\\infty}f(x) = 0\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty }g(x) = 0,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\n\\\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm\\infty}  \\dfrac {f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{x\\to \\pm\\infty}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)},\n$$\n\nprovided that $f$ and $g$ are both differentiable for all sufficiently large $x,$ and the limit $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to \\pm\\infty}  \\frac {f'(x)}{g'(x)}$ exists."] [:db/cas 17592186070015 :tutorial/content "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\textrm d x?$\n\nIn this case, the numerator is the derivative of the denominator! Again, integrals like this are always solvable by substitution. So, we let\n\n$$\n\\color{red}u = x^2+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad {\\color{blue}{\\textrm d u = 2x \\, \\textrm d x}},\n$$\n\nand we can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\operatorname{ln}|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|1+{x}^{2}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}(1+{x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\ln(1+x^2)+C.\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value bars because the function $1+x^2$ is always positive." "How do we calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\textrm d x?\n$$\n\nIn this case, the numerator is the derivative of the denominator! Again, integrals like this are always solvable by substitution. So, we let\n\n$$\n\\color{red}u = x^2+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad {\\color{blue}{\\textrm d u = 2x \\, \\textrm d x}},\n$$\n\nand we can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\operatorname{ln}|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|1+{x}^{2}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}(1+{x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\ln(1+x^2)+C.\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value bars because the function $1+x^2$ is always positive."] [:db/cas 17592186070017 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2x \\cos{(x^2)}\\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that if we let $u = x^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'} \\cos({\\overbrace{x^2}^u})\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the factor multiplying the trigonometric function is the derivative of the argument! Integrals like this can always be solved using substitution.\n\nSo, let $u=x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d u = 2x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that we want to calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x \\cos{(x^2)}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we let $u = x^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'} \\cos({\\overbrace{x^2}^u})\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the factor multiplying the trigonometric function is the derivative of the argument! Integrals like this can always be solved using substitution.\n\nSo, let $u=x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d u = 2x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186070031 :tutorial/content "We know that we can use the method of integration by parts to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ Now, suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2e^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ Can we use integration by parts in this case?\n\nLet's  try. We define $u$ and $v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {e}^{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for integration by parts leads to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})-\\int (\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe still don't have an answer, but the problem is reduced to the calculation of a simpler integral  $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ To compute this integral, we just need to apply the integration by parts formula again. For this second integral, we take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {e}^{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})-\\int (\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})+{C}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{4x}+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is a constant of integration.\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{4x}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{8}\\,x{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{32}\\,{e}^{4x}-\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\,{e}^{4x}(8{x}^{2}-4x+1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -\\dfrac{C_1}{2}.$" "We know that we can use the method of integration by parts to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int xe^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.\n$$ Now, suppose that we want to calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x^2e^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.\n$$ Can we use integration by parts in this case?\n\nLet's  try. We define $u$ and $v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {e}^{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for integration by parts leads to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})-\\int (\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe still don't have an answer, but the problem is reduced to the calculation of a simpler integral  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int xe^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.\n$$ To compute this integral, we just need to apply the integration by parts formula again. For this second integral, we take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {e}^{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})-\\int (\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})+{C}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{4x}+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is a constant of integration.\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{4x}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{8}\\,x{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{32}\\,{e}^{4x}-\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\,{e}^{4x}(8{x}^{2}-4x+1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -\\dfrac{C_1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186070035 :tutorial/content "How do we evaluate an integral like $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)} \\textrm{d}x?$\n\nFirst of all, note that the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions:\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)}=\\dfrac{A}{(x+2)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x-3)}\n$$\n\nIf we can figure out the partial fractions, then we can integrate each of the partial fractions using the natural logarithm.\n\nSo, let's compute the partial fractions. If we multiply the above equation by $(x+2)(x-3),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =A(x-3)+B(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above must hold for all values of $x.$ Plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)-1 & =A(-2-3)+B(-2+2) \\\\ -5 & =A(-5)+B(0) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)-1 & =A(3-3)+B(3+2) \\\\ 5 & =A(0)+B(5) \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)} = \\dfrac{1}{(x+2)} +\\dfrac{1}{(x-3)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can integrate each of the partial fractions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{1}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|x+2|+\\operatorname{ln}|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!" "How do we evaluate an integral like $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)} \\textrm{d}x?\n$$\n\nFirst of all, note that the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions:\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)}=\\dfrac{A}{(x+2)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x-3)}\n$$\n\nIf we can figure out the partial fractions, then we can integrate each of the partial fractions using the natural logarithm.\n\nSo, let's compute the partial fractions. If we multiply the above equation by $(x+2)(x-3),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =A(x-3)+B(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above must hold for all values of $x.$ Plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)-1 & =A(-2-3)+B(-2+2) \\\\ -5 & =A(-5)+B(0) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)-1 & =A(3-3)+B(3+2) \\\\ 5 & =A(0)+B(5) \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)} = \\dfrac{1}{(x+2)} +\\dfrac{1}{(x-3)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can integrate each of the partial fractions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x-1}{(x+2)(x-3)}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{1}{x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|x+2|+\\operatorname{ln}|x-3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!"] [:db/cas 17592186070036 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the integral  $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2} \\textrm{d}x.$ Note that we have the repeated factor $(x+1)$ in the denominator. \n\nThe correct form of the partial fraction expansion of the integrand is\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}=\\dfrac{A}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+1)^2} \\,.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =A(x+1)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)+2 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+2 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 2 & =A+B \\\\ 2 & =A-1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}= \\dfrac{3}{(x+1)} -\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nNow this function can be integrated term by term. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{(x+1)}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}|x+1|+\\frac{1}{x+1}+C\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant of integration." "Suppose we are given the integral  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2} \\textrm{d}x.\n$$ Note that we have the repeated factor $(x+1)$ in the denominator. \n\nThe correct form of the partial fraction expansion of the integrand is\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}=\\dfrac{A}{(x+1)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+1)^2} \\,.\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides the above equation by $(x+1)^2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =A(x+1)+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)+2 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+2 & =A(0+1)+B \\\\ 2 & =A+B \\\\ 2 & =A-1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x+2}{(x+1)^2}= \\dfrac{3}{(x+1)} -\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nNow this function can be integrated term by term. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{(x+1)}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}|x+1|+\\frac{1}{x+1}+C\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant of integration."] [:db/cas 17592186073423 :question/problem "![](images/dd/dde3a368edcdc45e82d671f5969e7529e99256062f24ca1996afd14be9eb20c4.png)\n\nUsing the area model above, determine an expression that could be missing from the left-hand side of the equation below.\n$\\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{\\dfrac{A}{A}\\times\\dfrac{A}{A}}} = \\dfrac{1}{24}$" "![](images/dd/dde3a368edcdc45e82d671f5969e7529e99256062f24ca1996afd14be9eb20c4.png)\n\nUsing the area model above, determine an expression that could be missing from the left-hand side of the equation below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{\\dfrac{A}{A}\\times\\dfrac{A}{A}}} = \\dfrac{1}{24}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073457 :question/worked-solution "First, we figure out the non-zero parts:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & ⟶2 \\\\ 70 & ⟶70\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that the second number has a block of zeros containing $1$ zero only.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{7} = \\color{red}{14}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the single $\\color{blue}0$:\n${\\color{red}14}{\\color{blue}0}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\times 70 = 140\n$$" "First, we figure out the non-zero parts:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}2 & ⟶2 \\\\ 70 & ⟶70\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that the second number has a block of zeros containing $1$ zero only.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{7} = \\color{red}{14}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the single $\\color{blue}0$:\n${\\color{red}14}{\\color{blue}0}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\times 70 = 140\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073458 :question/worked-solution "First, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3000 & ⟶3000 \\\\ 8 & ⟶8\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that there are $\\color{blue}3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{3} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{8} = \\color{red}{24}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of $\\color{blue}3$ zeros:\n${\\color{red}24}{\\color{blue}000}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8 \\times 3,000 = 24,000\n$$" "First, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3000 & ⟶3000 \\\\ 8 & ⟶8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $\\color{blue}3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{3} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{8} = \\color{red}{24}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of $\\color{blue}3$ zeros:\n${\\color{red}24}{\\color{blue}000}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8 \\times 3,000 = 24,000\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073459 :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total number of blueberries that the baker used, we need to multiply $50$ by $20$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}50 & ⟶50 \\\\ 20 & ⟶20\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that there are $\\color{blue}2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{5} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} = \\color{red}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}10}{\\color{blue}00}= 1,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, the baker used a total of $1,000$ blueberries to bake the muffins." "To calculate the total number of blueberries that the baker used, we need to multiply $50$ by $20$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}50 & ⟶50 \\\\ 20 & ⟶20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $\\color{blue}2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{5} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} = \\color{red}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}10}{\\color{blue}00}= 1,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, the baker used a total of $1,000$ blueberries to bake the muffins."] [:db/cas 17592186073460 :question/worked-solution "First, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}500 & ⟶500 \\\\ 3, 000 & ⟶3000\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that there are $\\color{blue}5$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{5} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{3} = {\\color{red}15}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n${\\color{red}15}{\\color{blue}00000}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n500 \\times 3,000 = 1,500,000\n$$" "First, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}500 & ⟶500 \\\\ 3, 000 & ⟶3000\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $\\color{blue}5$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{5} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{3} = {\\color{red}15}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n${\\color{red}15}{\\color{blue}00000}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n500 \\times 3,000 = 1,500,000\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073472 :question/problem "By writing both mixed numbers as improper fractions, determine the missing number in the following equality:\n\n$5 \\,\\dfrac{1}{9} - 4 \\,\\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}}{9}$" "By writing both mixed numbers as improper fractions, determine the missing number in the following equality:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 5 \\,\\dfrac{1}{9} - 4 \\,\\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{\\,\\boxed{ {\\phantom{0}}}}{9}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073474 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to subtract the fractions by putting them over a common denominator, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{8} - \\frac{1}{4} &= \\frac{1}{8} - \\frac{2 \\cdot 1}{2 \\cdot 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} - \\frac{2}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we cannot carry out this subtraction since the first fraction is *smaller* than the second!\n\nTo get around this problem, we write each mixed number as an improper fraction.\n\n- Writing $3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8} = \\dfrac{(3 \\times 8) + 1}{8} = \\dfrac{25}{8}$.\n- Writing $1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 4) + 1}{4} = \\dfrac{5}{4}$.\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{8} - \\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can make a common denominator of $8$. To put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $8$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4} = \\dfrac{5 \\times 2}{4 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{10}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{8} - \\dfrac{10}{8} = \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{8} = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 7 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{7}{8}\n$$" "If we attempt to subtract the fractions by putting them over a common denominator, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{8} - \\frac{1}{4} &= \\frac{1}{8} - \\frac{2 \\cdot 1}{2 \\cdot 4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} - \\frac{2}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we cannot carry out this subtraction since the first fraction is *smaller* than the second!\n\nTo get around this problem, we write each mixed number as an improper fraction.\n\n- Writing $3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8} = \\dfrac{(3 \\times 8) + 1}{8} = \\dfrac{25}{8}.\n$$\n- Writing $1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{(1 \\times 4) + 1}{4} = \\dfrac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{8} - \\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can make a common denominator of $8$. To put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $8$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4} = \\dfrac{5 \\times 2}{4 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{10}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{8} - \\dfrac{10}{8} = \\dfrac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{8} = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} \\, 7 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{7}{8}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073478 :question/worked-solution "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$206.6\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 3.43 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 207 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}3$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n\n$$\n207 \\div 3\n$$\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $6$$9$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$$2$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $206.6 \\div 3.43$ is approximately equal to $69$." "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 206.6\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 3.43 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 207 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n\n$$\n207 \\div 3\n$$\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $6$$9$$3$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$8$$2$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$7$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $206.6 \\div 3.43$ is approximately equal to $69$."] [:db/cas 17592186073479 :question/worked-solution "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$116.8\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 5.15 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 117 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}5$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n$117\\div 5$.\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n|  |  | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $1$ | $7$ |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n|  |  |  | $\\fbox{2}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n117 \\div 5 = 23\\,\\text{R}2\n$$\n\nThe remainder $2$ is closer to $0$ than to the divisor $5$. So, to round to the nearest whole number, we must round down:\n\n$$\n117 \\div 5 \\rightarrow 23\n$$\n\nTherefore, $116.8 \\div 5.15$ is approximately equal to $23$." "We round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$$\n\\displaystyle 116.8\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 5.15 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 117 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}5\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the value of\n$117\\div 5$.\n\nSo, we go through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n|  |  | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |  |\n| $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |\n|  |  | $1$ | $7$ |\n|  | $-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n|  |  |  | $\\fbox{2}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n117 \\div 5 = 23\\,\\text{R}2\n$$\n\nThe remainder $2$ is closer to $0$ than to the divisor $5$. So, to round to the nearest whole number, we must round down:\n\n$$\n117 \\div 5 \\rightarrow 23\n$$\n\nTherefore, $116.8 \\div 5.15$ is approximately equal to $23$."] [:db/cas 17592186073491 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4491897e98f32e3ebc352f7012541f3aec062299ab8ea788210aba1dd2fbd7b.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the statement below.\n\n$2 - \\dfrac{3}{5} = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{5}$" "![](images/a4/a4491897e98f32e3ebc352f7012541f3aec062299ab8ea788210aba1dd2fbd7b.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the statement below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2 - \\dfrac{3}{5} = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{5}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073505 :question/problem "![](images/76/76a30d70a6c69ce4375e108f9a22d8bc8bbe2f36077342a46dcfb226bd9b58cd.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the following statement:\n$\\dfrac{5}{6} + 1 = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{6}$" "![](images/76/76a30d70a6c69ce4375e108f9a22d8bc8bbe2f36077342a46dcfb226bd9b58cd.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the following statement:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{5}{6} + 1 = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{6}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073544 :question/problem "![](images/e5/e52880d8db4da89f583cd3fe5323b7f7c551b5a4e2a5b657aeed7177ec367827.png)\n\nThe equation below gives the area of the shaded region. From *left to right*, what numbers are missing?\n\n$\\textrm{Area} = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{3} \\times \\dfrac{3}{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}} = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{12}$" "![](images/e5/e52880d8db4da89f583cd3fe5323b7f7c551b5a4e2a5b657aeed7177ec367827.png)\n\nThe equation below gives the area of the shaded region. From *left to right*, what numbers are missing?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{Area} = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{3} \\times \\dfrac{3}{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}} = \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}{12}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073544 :question/worked-solution "First, notice the unit square is divided into $3$ equal slices horizontally and into $4$ slices vertically. So, each of the small tiles is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nunits long and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nunits high.\n\nThe shaded region is $2$ tiles long and $3$ tiles high, which means that it has dimensions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nunits by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nunits.\n\n![](images/de/defd66d84d10e351b131828d86d26c04bdf2792aa003355e5cf6910e65d1cff7.png)\n\nWe find the area of the shaded rectangle by multiplying the lengths of the sides:\n$\\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{blue}2 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}}{3} \\times \\dfrac{3}{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{red}4 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}} =\\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{magenta}6 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}}{12}$\n\nSo, from left to right, the missing numbers are ${\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}4}$ and $\\color{magenta}6$." "First, notice the unit square is divided into $3$ equal slices horizontally and into $4$ slices vertically. So, each of the small tiles is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nunits long and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nunits high.\n\nThe shaded region is $2$ tiles long and $3$ tiles high, which means that it has dimensions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nunits by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nunits.\n\n![](images/de/defd66d84d10e351b131828d86d26c04bdf2792aa003355e5cf6910e65d1cff7.png)\n\nWe find the area of the shaded rectangle by multiplying the lengths of the sides:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{blue}2 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}}{3} \\times \\dfrac{3}{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{red}4 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}} =\\dfrac{\\boxed{ {\\hspace{0.167em} {\\color{magenta}6 \\hspace{0.167em}}}}}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, from left to right, the missing numbers are ${\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}4}$ and $\\color{magenta}6$."] [:db/cas 17592186073593 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, $\\triangle OBC$ and $\\triangle OBA$ are congruent by SSS (side-side-side).\n\nIndeed, $\\overline{OB}$ is the common side of the triangles, and we have the following additional congruent pairs of sides:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}AB^(―) & ≅BC^(―) \\\\ OA^(―) & ≅OC^(―)\\end{bmatrix}$" "From the diagram, $\\triangle OBC$ and $\\triangle OBA$ are congruent by SSS (side-side-side).\n\nIndeed, $\\overline{OB}$ is the common side of the triangles, and we have the following additional congruent pairs of sides:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}AB^(―) & ≅BC^(―) \\\\ OA^(―) & ≅OC^(―)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073598 :question/worked-solution "Given two similar polygons, the similarity ratio is the ratio of any two corresponding sides. \n\nNotice that, for example, $\\overline{LM}$ is the longest side of $NLMP.$ Hence, it corresponds to the longest side of $ABCD,$ which is  $\\overline{BC}.$ \n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{BC}{LM} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The ratio of the smaller polygon to the larger polygon would be $\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" "Given two similar polygons, the similarity ratio is the ratio of any two corresponding sides. \n\nNotice that, for example, $\\overline{LM}$ is the longest side of $NLMP.$ Hence, it corresponds to the longest side of $ABCD,$ which is  $\\overline{BC}.$ \n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{BC}{LM} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The ratio of the smaller polygon to the larger polygon would be $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073652 :question/worked-solution "Partitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:3$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:3\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+3 = 4$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{4}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly below $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](images/29/29b01b5cc8cca113a14c16f95626faadb70993bbb42fab7fd77f011ecf0de734.png)\n\nTo get from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$x_{C}|= x_{A} + \\frac{1}{4}(x_{B} - x_{A}); = 2 + \\frac{1}{4}(6 - 2); = 2 + 1; = 3$.\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$y_{C}|= y_{A} + \\frac{1}{4}(y_{B} - y_{A}); = 1 + \\frac{1}{4}(9 - 1); = 1 + 2; = 3$.\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 3 \\right)\n$$" "Partitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:3$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:3\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+3 = 4$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{4}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly below $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](images/29/29b01b5cc8cca113a14c16f95626faadb70993bbb42fab7fd77f011ecf0de734.png)\n\nTo get from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_{C}|= x_{A} + \\frac{1}{4}(x_{B} - x_{A}); = 2 + \\frac{1}{4}(6 - 2); = 2 + 1; = 3.\n$$\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle y_{C}|= y_{A} + \\frac{1}{4}(y_{B} - y_{A}); = 1 + \\frac{1}{4}(9 - 1); = 1 + 2; = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 3 \\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073994 :question/problem "By writing both mixed numbers as improper fractions, determine the missing number in the following equality:\n\n$9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}} - 7 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{20}}$" "By writing both mixed numbers as improper fractions, determine the missing number in the following equality:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}} - 7 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{5}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{20}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186073997 :question/problem "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3p + q &=-9 \\\\ 5p + 4q &=32\\end{aligned}\\right.$." "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3p + q &=-9 \\\\ 5p + 4q &=32\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$."] [:db/cas 17592186073998 :question/problem "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-7a + 2b &=18 \\\\ -6a + 6b &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$" "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-7a + 2b &=18 \\\\ -6a + 6b &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074022 :question/problem "![](images/39/395914ec574a4ea4e0990f7400e4c5f7832925d0d9688c55b8fda0808c098632.png)\n\nUsing the area model above, determine which expression could be missing from the left-hand side of the equation below.\n$\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{A}{A}} \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{A}{A}}}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{12}}$" "![](images/39/395914ec574a4ea4e0990f7400e4c5f7832925d0d9688c55b8fda0808c098632.png)\n\nUsing the area model above, determine which expression could be missing from the left-hand side of the equation below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{A}{A}} \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{A}{A}}}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{12}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074023 :question/problem "![](images/a1/a136c99291c1400ced5ec78aeee5ef2a8dd8bae1a3d51aeee546b36ace99b53f.png)\n\nUsing the area model above, determine which expression could be missing from the left-hand side of the equation below.\n$\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{A}{A}} \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{A}{A}}}} = 21$" "![](images/a1/a136c99291c1400ced5ec78aeee5ef2a8dd8bae1a3d51aeee546b36ace99b53f.png)\n\nUsing the area model above, determine which expression could be missing from the left-hand side of the equation below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{ {\\displaystyle \\frac{A}{A}} \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{A}{A}}}} = 21\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074030 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x + 5y &=13 \\\\ y &=x + 5\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$" "Consider the following system of equations:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x + 5y &=13 \\\\ y &=x + 5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$"] [:db/cas 17592186074047 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p - q$?\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5q + 55 &=0 \\\\ 2p + 3q + 3 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$" "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p - q$?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5q + 55 &=0 \\\\ 2p + 3q + 3 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074172 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3a + 2b &=4 \\\\ - 4a + b &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.$" "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3a + 2b &=4 \\\\ - 4a + b &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074181 :question/problem "![](images/2e/2e818f399be08fed229a82e41b8caff916b70e7298a28f90a182c7e62a741c3e.png)\n\nThe triangle shown above is transformed subsequently by functions $f_{1}(x, y) ↦ (\\frac{x}{2}, \\frac{y}{2})$ and $f_{2}(x, y) ↦ (x,-y)$. What is the resulting triangle?" "![](images/2e/2e818f399be08fed229a82e41b8caff916b70e7298a28f90a182c7e62a741c3e.png)\n\nThe triangle shown above is transformed subsequently by functions $$\n\\displaystyle f_{1}(x, y) ↦ (\\frac{x}{2}, \\frac{y}{2})\n$$ and $f_{2}(x, y) ↦ (x,-y)$. What is the resulting triangle?"] [:db/cas 17592186074273 :question/problem "By writing both mixed numbers as improper fractions, determine the missing number in the following equality:\n\n$3 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{8}} - 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{8}}$" "By writing both mixed numbers as improper fractions, determine the missing number in the following equality:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 3 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{8}} - 1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{4}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\hspace{0.167em} \\boxed{\\phantom{0}}}{8}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074335 :question/problem "![](images/50/502be61a529cd2c5430b76f70e5328a00650ac5744ea0da7c48dc22853f4b697.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the statement below.\n${\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} + 2 = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{3}}$" "![](images/50/502be61a529cd2c5430b76f70e5328a00650ac5744ea0da7c48dc22853f4b697.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the statement below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} + 2 = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{3}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074339 :question/problem "![](images/68/687a48022f9f1885d4a23f8cc2a0accf8e0955f97003a988b25d5b343722a53e.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the statement below.\n$1 - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{6}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{6}}$" "![](images/68/687a48022f9f1885d4a23f8cc2a0accf8e0955f97003a988b25d5b343722a53e.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the statement below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1 - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{6}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{6}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074348 :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b46e3d38cf957f8e1a411eb9fa293018e21f85c6db286c147b28597183dffa2.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the statement below.\n$1 + {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{9}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{9}}$" "![](images/0b/0b46e3d38cf957f8e1a411eb9fa293018e21f85c6db286c147b28597183dffa2.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the statement below.\n$$\n\\displaystyle 1 + {\\displaystyle \\frac{7}{9}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{9}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074349 :question/problem "![](images/00/00d0ced7036634e419dd43dd34a8284c7ed9fc1550b561cdf7f17ca90b3d9a87.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the statement below.\n\n$2 - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{9}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{9}}$" "![](images/00/00d0ced7036634e419dd43dd34a8284c7ed9fc1550b561cdf7f17ca90b3d9a87.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the statement below.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2 - {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{9}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{9}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074366 :question/problem "![](images/9c/9ca0fd81b2515d34505154f372300500fd68cd64171ccbc0aa2bf070c4a50077.png)\n\nEach of the two shapes in the model above represents one whole. From left to right, what numbers are missing from the division problem below?\n$\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}} \\div  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} = \\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}$" "![](images/9c/9ca0fd81b2515d34505154f372300500fd68cd64171ccbc0aa2bf070c4a50077.png)\n\nEach of the two shapes in the model above represents one whole. From left to right, what numbers are missing from the division problem below?\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}} \\div  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4}} = \\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074453 :question/problem "![](images/24/24f129edf776e7bb93ab61ea82acbec2ae901d45babfdcd23f87d22885bac6e3.png)\n\nThe equation below gives the area of the shaded region. From *left to right*, what numbers are missing?\n\n$\\text{Area} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{5}} \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{20}}$" "![](images/24/24f129edf776e7bb93ab61ea82acbec2ae901d45babfdcd23f87d22885bac6e3.png)\n\nThe equation below gives the area of the shaded region. From *left to right*, what numbers are missing?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Area} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{5}} \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{20}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074459 :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f72b74f03039aee60a3633415a24ecae1f574e558f72b8011b4b59b4fe62d71.png)\n\nThe equation below gives the area of the shaded region. From *left to right*, what numbers are missing?\n\n$\\text{Area} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}} \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{5}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{9}{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}$" "![](images/3f/3f72b74f03039aee60a3633415a24ecae1f574e558f72b8011b4b59b4fe62d71.png)\n\nThe equation below gives the area of the shaded region. From *left to right*, what numbers are missing?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Area} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}} \\times  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}{5}} = {\\displaystyle \\frac{9}{\\boxed{\\hspace{0.167em} \\phantom{0} \\hspace{0.167em}}}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186074977 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the following right triangle.\n\n![](images/46/466ec5d41b5e06a54cef9e1d840ad6ef0b22a479b81fa56b9612eb8cc03deae2.png)\n\nHow can we find its area?\n\nIt turns out that:\n\n> The area of a right triangle is half the area of the corresponding rectangle:\n> $\\text{Area of Triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\text{(Area of Rectangle)}$.\n\nIn our case, the corresponding rectangle has dimensions $8\\:\\text{m}$ by $6\\:\\text{m}$.\n\n![](images/4d/4d2dbaba3d6a4064901bb4dd9bd444c32ef1163f21d9e6cbec2ac5cf5962c39b.png)\n\nThe area of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Rectangle &= 8m \\cdot 6m \\\\\n&= (8 \\cdot 6)(m \\cdot m) \\\\\n&= 48m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area of Triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 48 = 24 \\: \\text{m}^2\n$$\n\nLet’s see more examples." "Let's look at the following right triangle.\n\n![](images/46/466ec5d41b5e06a54cef9e1d840ad6ef0b22a479b81fa56b9612eb8cc03deae2.png)\n\nHow can we find its area?\n\nIt turns out that:\n\n> The area of a right triangle is half the area of the corresponding rectangle:\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Area of Triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\text{(Area of Rectangle)}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the corresponding rectangle has dimensions $8\\:\\text{m}$ by $6\\:\\text{m}$.\n\n![](images/4d/4d2dbaba3d6a4064901bb4dd9bd444c32ef1163f21d9e6cbec2ac5cf5962c39b.png)\n\nThe area of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea of Rectangle &= 8m \\cdot 6m \\\\\n&= (8 \\cdot 6)(m \\cdot m) \\\\\n&= 48m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area of Triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 48 = 24 \\: \\text{m}^2\n$$\n\nLet’s see more examples."] [:db/cas 17592186075008 :tutorial/content "We can partition any segment in a given ratio using techniques like those discussed for horizontal and vertical segments.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the point $C$ on the segment from the point $A(-1,0)$ to the point $B(7,4)$ that divides the segment into the ratio $1:1$.\n\n![](images/8e/8e67f8d03c2238fa342b98a58fd2527419f6f52ea98543eb85247d4c3706352a.png)\n\nPartitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:1$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:1\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+1 = 2$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{2}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly above $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](images/1e/1e2d40e0648ba95a734dd9166852907759c8f50af06653d7b1a34785e1944d6a.png)\n\nTo go from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$.\n\nSo, we compute the coordinates of $C$ as follows:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$x_{C}|= x_{A} + \\frac{1}{2}(x_{B} - x_{A}); =-1 + \\frac{1}{2}(7 - (-1)); =-1 + \\frac{8}{2}; = 3$.\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$y_{C}|= y_{A} + \\frac{1}{2}(y_{B} - y_{A}); = 0 + \\frac{1}{2}(4 - 0); = \\frac{4}{2}; = 2$.\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 2 \\right)\n$$" "We can partition any segment in a given ratio using techniques like those discussed for horizontal and vertical segments.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the point $C$ on the segment from the point $A(-1,0)$ to the point $B(7,4)$ that divides the segment into the ratio $1:1$.\n\n![](images/8e/8e67f8d03c2238fa342b98a58fd2527419f6f52ea98543eb85247d4c3706352a.png)\n\nPartitioning a line segment $\\overline{AB}$ into a ratio $a:b$ means we must\n\n- divide the segment into $a+b$ equal parts, and\n- find the point $C$ that is $a$ equal parts from $A$.\n\nIn our case, the ratio $1:1$ implies\n\n$$\nAC:CB = 1:1\n$$\n\nThis means\n\n- there are $1+1 = 2$ equal parts, and\n- we want to find the point $C$ that is $1$ equal part from $A$. So, we have\n$AC= \\dfrac{1}{2}AB$.\n\nNext, consider another point $X$ that is horizontal with $A$ and directly above $C$, as shown in the diagram.\n\n![](images/1e/1e2d40e0648ba95a734dd9166852907759c8f50af06653d7b1a34785e1944d6a.png)\n\nTo go from point $A$ to point $C$, we can \"run\" horizontally from $A$ to $X$ and then \"rise\" vertically from $X$ to $C$.\n\nSo, we compute the coordinates of $C$ as follows:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_{C}|= x_{A} + \\frac{1}{2}(x_{B} - x_{A}); =-1 + \\frac{1}{2}(7 - (-1)); =-1 + \\frac{8}{2}; = 3.\n$$\n- The $y$-coordinate of $C$ is\n$$\n\\displaystyle y_{C}|= y_{A} + \\frac{1}{2}(y_{B} - y_{A}); = 0 + \\frac{1}{2}(4 - 0); = \\frac{4}{2}; = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C$ has coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 2 \\right)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186075027 :tutorial/content "Consider the line $l$ with the equation $y=-x + 6$ and the point $Q(2,1)$ that does not lie on $l.$\n\n![](images/83/8353825890413432e74d30512219cb95edc76fe8c24e7a824d6893c745c48bd1.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find the point $P$ that lies on $l$ that's closest to the point $(2,1).$ \n\n![](images/f8/f880cfde6bdfe07c6804609f9b052189632afcde4907207de5245298aea9ea6e.png)\n\nNow, here's the key idea: $P$ is the point that lies on $l$ *and* lies on the line *perpendicular* to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\nSo, to find $P,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the line perpendicular to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\n- Then, we find the intersection of the two lines.\n\nThe line $y=-x + 6$ has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1$ (the negative reciprocal). It also must pass through the point $(2,1).$ So, using point-slope form, we find that the equation of the perpendicular line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =x-2 \\\\ y & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the lines $y=-x+6$ and $y=x-1$ intersect, we set the equations equal to each other and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+6 & =x-1 \\\\ 6+1 & =x+x \\\\ 7 & =2x \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{7}{2}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ So, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left( \\dfrac{7}{2}, \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right).$" "Consider the line $l$ with the equation $y=-x + 6$ and the point $Q(2,1)$ that does not lie on $l.$\n\n![](images/83/8353825890413432e74d30512219cb95edc76fe8c24e7a824d6893c745c48bd1.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find the point $P$ that lies on $l$ that's closest to the point $(2,1).$ \n\n![](images/f8/f880cfde6bdfe07c6804609f9b052189632afcde4907207de5245298aea9ea6e.png)\n\nNow, here's the key idea: $P$ is the point that lies on $l$ *and* lies on the line *perpendicular* to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\nSo, to find $P,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the line perpendicular to $l$ that passes through $Q.$\n\n- Then, we find the intersection of the two lines.\n\nThe line $y=-x + 6$ has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1$ (the negative reciprocal). It also must pass through the point $(2,1).$ So, using point-slope form, we find that the equation of the perpendicular line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =x-2 \\\\ y & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the lines $y=-x+6$ and $y=x-1$ intersect, we set the equations equal to each other and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+6 & =x-1 \\\\ 6+1 & =x+x \\\\ 7 & =2x \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{7}{2}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\dfrac{5}{2}.$ So, the coordinates of $P$ are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left( \\dfrac{7}{2}, \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186075039 :tutorial/content "We can use the method of counting zeros to multiply a number that ends in zero by another number.\n\nFor example, to find $80 \\times 4$, we first figure out the non-zero parts:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}80 & ⟶80 \\\\ 4 & ⟶4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that the first number has a block of zeros containing $1$ zero only.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{8} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{32}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join this product with the single $\\color{blue}0$.\n${\\color{red}32}{\\color{blue}0}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n80\\times 4 = 320\n$$" "We can use the method of counting zeros to multiply a number that ends in zero by another number.\n\nFor example, to find $80 \\times 4$, we first figure out the non-zero parts:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}80 & ⟶80 \\\\ 4 & ⟶4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that the first number has a block of zeros containing $1$ zero only.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{8} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{32}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join this product with the single $\\color{blue}0$.\n${\\color{red}32}{\\color{blue}0}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n80\\times 4 = 320\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186075040 :tutorial/content "We can use the method of counting zeros to multiply a number that ends in multiple zeros by another number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's multiply $9\\times400$. First, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}9 & ⟶9 \\\\ 400 & ⟶400\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that the second number has a block containing $\\color{blue}2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{9} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of $\\color{blue}2$ zeros:\n${\\color{red}36}{\\color{blue}00}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n9 \\times 400 = 3,600\n$$" "We can use the method of counting zeros to multiply a number that ends in multiple zeros by another number.\n\nTo illustrate, let's multiply $9\\times400$. First, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}9 & ⟶9 \\\\ 400 & ⟶400\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that the second number has a block containing $\\color{blue}2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{9} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of $\\color{blue}2$ zeros:\n${\\color{red}36}{\\color{blue}00}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n9 \\times 400 = 3,600\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186075041 :tutorial/content "We can use the method of counting zeros to multiply two numbers that both end with zero.\n\nFor example, to multiply $60$ by $70$, first, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}60 & ⟶60 \\\\ 70 & ⟶70\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that there are $\\color{blue}2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{6} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{7} = \\color{red}{42}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}42}{\\color{blue}00}= 4,200\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n70\\times 60 = 4,200\n$$" "We can use the method of counting zeros to multiply two numbers that both end with zero.\n\nFor example, to multiply $60$ by $70$, first, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}60 & ⟶60 \\\\ 70 & ⟶70\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $\\color{blue}2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{6} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{7} = \\color{red}{42}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}42}{\\color{blue}00}= 4,200\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n70\\times 60 = 4,200\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186075042 :tutorial/content "We can use the method of counting zeros to multiply numbers that both end in multiple zeros.\n\nFor instance, let's calculate $600 \\times 70$. First, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}600 & ⟶600 \\\\ 70 & ⟶70\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that there are $\\color{blue}3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{6} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{7} = {\\color{red}42}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n${\\color{red}42}{\\color{blue}000}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n600 \\times 70 = 42,000\n$$" "We can use the method of counting zeros to multiply numbers that both end in multiple zeros.\n\nFor instance, let's calculate $600 \\times 70$. First, we figure out the non-zero part and the block of zeros for both numbers:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}600 & ⟶600 \\\\ 70 & ⟶70\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can see that there are $\\color{blue}3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{6} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{7} = {\\color{red}42}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n${\\color{red}42}{\\color{blue}000}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n600 \\times 70 = 42,000\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078147 :question/problem "Prove that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)$ using mathematical induction." "Prove that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)\n$$ using mathematical induction."] [:db/cas 17592186078147 :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3),\\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{{4}^{1}}=\\frac{1}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{1}})=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n = k \\geq 1.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k = 1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = k \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^k}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^{k+1}}\\right). \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} a_i =  \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_i\\right) + a_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}} & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}})+\\left(\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}})+\\left(\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}})+\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {4}^{k}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}\\left(\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)$ is true for all $n\\geq 1.$" "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3),\\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{{4}^{1}}=\\frac{1}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{1}})=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n = k \\geq 1.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k = 1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = k \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^k}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^{k+1}}\\right). \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} a_i =  \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_i\\right) + a_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}} & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}})+\\left(\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}})+\\left(\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}})+\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {4}^{k}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}\\left(\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)\n$$ is true for all $n\\geq 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078148 :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle S(k) = \\sum_{i=1}^k 2^i,$ express $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k).$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle S(k) = \\sum_{i=1}^k 2^i,\n$$ express $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k).$"] [:db/cas 17592186078149 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n 3i(i+3) = n(n+1)(n+5)\n$$\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template below?\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}3i(i+3)=\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^k 3i(i+3) = \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{k(k+1)(k+5)}} .\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} 3i(i+3) = \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{(k+1)(k+2)(k+6)}} .\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n 3i(i+3) = n(n+1)(n+5)$ for all $n\\geq 1.$" "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n 3i(i+3) = n(n+1)(n+5)\n$$\n\nWhat are the missing entries in the proof template below?\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}3i(i+3)=\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^k 3i(i+3) = \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{k(k+1)(k+5)}} .\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} 3i(i+3) = \\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{(k+1)(k+2)(k+6)}} .\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n 3i(i+3) = n(n+1)(n+5)\n$$ for all $n\\geq 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078149 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n 3i(i+3) = n(n+1)(n+5)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}3i(i+3)=1\\cdot (1+1)(1+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^k 3i(i+3) = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k(k+1)(k+5)}.\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} 3i(i+3) = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(k+1)(k+2)(k+6)}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n 3i(i+3) = n(n+1)(n+5)$ for all $n\\geq 1.$" "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n 3i(i+3) = n(n+1)(n+5)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}3i(i+3)=1\\cdot (1+1)(1+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\geq 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^k 3i(i+3) = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}k(k+1)(k+5)}.\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} 3i(i+3) = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}(k+1)(k+2)(k+6)}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n 3i(i+3) = n(n+1)(n+5)\n$$ for all $n\\geq 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078175 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the corresponding line in the slope-intercept form\n\n$$\ny=mx+b\n$$\n\n- The line intersects the $y$-axis at $(0,4)$, so the $y$-intercept is $b=4$.\n- The line passes through the points $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$, so its slope is\n$m = \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} = \\frac{0 - 4}{2 - 0} = \\frac{-4}{2} =-2$.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=-2x+4\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is *below* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line.\n\nSo, the corresponding inequality is\n$y < -2x+4$." "First, we find the equation of the corresponding line in the slope-intercept form\n\n$$\ny=mx+b\n$$\n\n- The line intersects the $y$-axis at $(0,4)$, so the $y$-intercept is $b=4$.\n- The line passes through the points $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$, so its slope is\n$$\n\\displaystyle m = \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} = \\frac{0 - 4}{2 - 0} = \\frac{-4}{2} =-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=-2x+4\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is *below* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line.\n\nSo, the corresponding inequality is\n$y < -2x+4$."] [:db/cas 17592186078201 :question/problem "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $2, \\: 4, \\: 8, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$\n\nII. $1, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\: \\dfrac{1}{9}, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{27}, \\: \\ldots$\n\nIII. $4, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$" "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $2, \\: 4, \\: 8, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle 1, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\: \\dfrac{1}{9}, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{27}, \\: \\ldots\n$$\n\nIII. $4, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$"] [:db/cas 17592186078215 :question/problem "If $D(y)=y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} - \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} + y^3,$ then calculate $D(e^{3x}).$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle D(y)=y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} - \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} + y^3,\n$$ then calculate $D(e^{3x}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186078216 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x^{100}}{e^x}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x^{100}}{e^x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078217 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x}{\\ln{x}}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{x}{\\ln{x}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078218 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{e^{2x}}{\\ln{x}}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{e^{2x}}{\\ln{x}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078219 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078229 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle {\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2x^2-3x}}{x^2+x}}$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2x^2-3x}}{x^2+x}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078276 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below?\n\n1. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2}f(x)=0$\n2. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n3. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)$\n\n![](images/de/dea690cf5bae7ef2463bd2f274bbc50becaa6d24f8d4aed926d9c0fda7f5e6d1.png)" "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2}f(x)=0\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)\n$$\n\n![](images/de/dea690cf5bae7ef2463bd2f274bbc50becaa6d24f8d4aed926d9c0fda7f5e6d1.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186078276 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We see from the graph that $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2^-}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2^+}f(x)=0$. Consequently, $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2}f(x)=0$.\n- Statement II is true. When approaching $x=1$ from the left and from the right, we get\n$\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)=0, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)=3$,\nrespectively. Since $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)\\ne\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$, we conclude that $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$.\n- Statement III is false. Both limits $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $[-1,0)$. We *cannot* say that the limits are equal because we can't compare the undefined values.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We see from the graph that $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2^-}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2^+}f(x)=0.\n$$ Consequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2}f(x)=0.\n$$\n- Statement II is true. When approaching $x=1$ from the left and from the right, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)=0, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)=3,\n$$\nrespectively. Since $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)\\ne\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$, we conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n- Statement III is false. Both limits $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $[-1,0)$. We *cannot* say that the limits are equal because we can't compare the undefined values.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."] [:db/cas 17592186078279 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{e^{3+h} - e^3}{h}$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{e^{3+h} - e^3}{h}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078280 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\pi/3}\\dfrac{\\left(\\sin(x) - \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(x-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\pi/3}\\dfrac{\\left(\\sin(x) - \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(x-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078281 :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ shown below?\n\n1. ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{f(-4+h) -f(-4)}{h}=2}$\n2. ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{f(2+h) -f(2)}{h} =\\dfrac{1}{2}}$\n3. ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{f(4+h) -f(4)}{h} =-1}$\n\n![](images/62/6279e3f40dd65e80550b27a2fbf2ed709fb72396f34ab605f310b9d348140b3c.png)" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ shown below?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{f(-4+h) -f(-4)}{h}=2}\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{f(2+h) -f(2)}{h} =\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\dfrac{f(4+h) -f(4)}{h} =-1}\n$$\n\n![](images/62/6279e3f40dd65e80550b27a2fbf2ed709fb72396f34ab605f310b9d348140b3c.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186078287 :question/problem "What is the value of  $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^3 5^n}?$" "What is the value of  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^3 5^n}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078289 :question/problem "Express $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=2}^{4} (2n-3)$ as a sum of three terms." "Express $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=2}^{4} (2n-3)\n$$ as a sum of three terms."] [:db/cas 17592186078290 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_\\pi^\\pi \\sqrt{\\sin x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078291 :question/problem "Given that ${\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6} \\, f(x) \\; \\textrm d x = -54 },$ what is the value of\n${\\displaystyle \\int_{6}^{0} \\bigg( \\dfrac 1 3 f(x) + 1 \\bigg) \\; \\textrm d x }?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6} \\, f(x) \\; \\textrm d x = -54 },\n$$ what is the value of\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\int_{6}^{0} \\bigg( \\dfrac 1 3 f(x) + 1 \\bigg) \\; \\textrm d x }?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078292 :question/problem "What is the sum of the arithmetic series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^{25} (2n + 1)}$?" "What is the sum of the arithmetic series $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^{25} (2n + 1)}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186078295 :question/problem "If $\\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 18$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = -6$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{10} \\big( 2f(x)+1 \\big) \\,\\textrm d x?$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 18\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = -6\n$$ then what is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{10} \\big( 2f(x)+1 \\big) \\,\\textrm d x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078304 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\displaystyle \\int_4^1 2 \\, f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = -1}$ \nand ${\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{-1} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2}.$  What is the value of ${\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^4 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx}?$" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\int_4^1 2 \\, f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = -1}\n$$ \nand $$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{-1} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2}.\n$$  What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^4 f(x) \\, \\textrm dx}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078309 :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?$\n$$\nf(x)= \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}3 , & x < 0 \\\\ 2 x + 3 , & x \\ge  0\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?\n$$\n$$\nf(x)= \n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}3 , & x < 0 \\\\ 2 x + 3 , & x \\ge  0\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078310 :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad45cbdcd126df931f1a35c60ca12b45d520a2a0a5cddf4fa012c0515d3024c7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x.$" "![](images/ad/ad45cbdcd126df931f1a35c60ca12b45d520a2a0a5cddf4fa012c0515d3024c7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078311 :question/problem "![](images/36/36c6a1c84e0b0c32a5fd35fd422645f60340da6d205dd8cc31054cd558c2f5f9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "![](images/36/36c6a1c84e0b0c32a5fd35fd422645f60340da6d205dd8cc31054cd558c2f5f9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078312 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_2^x \\dfrac {t^3} {t-1}\\,\\textrm d t$ for $x>2.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_2^x \\dfrac {t^3} {t-1}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$ for $x>2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078313 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0  \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0  \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078314 :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ for $f(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_2^{x^2}\\arctan t\\,\\textrm d t.$" "Find $f'(x)$ for $$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_2^{x^2}\\arctan t\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078315 :question/problem "Find $f'(1)$ if  $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\int_x^{x^2} (t^2 + 2t)  \\,\\, \\textrm d t.$" "Find $f'(1)$ if  $$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\int_x^{x^2} (t^2 + 2t)  \\,\\, \\textrm d t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078316 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^+} \\dfrac{15x}{\\mid x^3+x \\mid}$." "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-2)^+} \\dfrac{15x}{\\mid x^3+x \\mid}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078329 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta \\cot \\theta}{4 \\cos^2 \\theta}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\theta \\cot \\theta}{4 \\cos^2 \\theta}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078335 :question/problem "Given that $D=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}}{\\textrm d x^{2}}+3 \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}+5,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=e^{- x}?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}}{\\textrm d x^{2}}+3 \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}+5,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=e^{- x}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186078336 :question/problem "Given that $D=x^2\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}}{\\textrm d x^{3}}-5x \\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}}{\\textrm d x^{2}} +9\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\ln{x}?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=x^2\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}}{\\textrm d x^{3}}-5x \\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}}{\\textrm d x^{2}} +9\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x},\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\ln{x}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186078343 :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty\\dfrac{3(2)^{kn}}{4^n}$ converge?" "For which values of $k$ does the series $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty\\dfrac{3(2)^{kn}}{4^n}\n$$ converge?"] [:db/cas 17592186078343 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a r^n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(2)^{kn}}{{4}^{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3\\frac{{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{\\left(\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r = \\dfrac{2^k}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r| < 1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {2}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\dfrac{2^k}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation $2^k = 4$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since $2^k$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to $2^k < 4$ must be $k < 2.$" "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form $$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a r^n,\n$$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(2)^{kn}}{{4}^{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3\\frac{{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{\\left(\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4}\\right)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r = \\dfrac{2^k}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r| < 1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {2}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\dfrac{2^k}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation $2^k = 4$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since $2^k$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to $2^k < 4$ must be $k < 2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078344 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow 1^+}\\dfrac{\\mid x-1 \\mid}{\\sqrt{x-1}}$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow 1^+}\\dfrac{\\mid x-1 \\mid}{\\sqrt{x-1}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078346 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0^-} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2}}{x}$." "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0^-} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x^2}}{x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078348 :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$" "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078348 :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4(-3)^{2}}{4(-3)^{1}} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$ starts with $n=6.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{6}} & =\\frac{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4(7)}}{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4(6)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{28}}{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{24}} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| < 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{1+3}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{0+3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{4}^{0}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges." "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}\n$$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4(-3)^{2}}{4(-3)^{1}} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\n- The series $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}\n$$ starts with $n=6.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{6}} & =\\frac{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4(7)}}{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4(6)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{28}}{32{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{24}} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| < 1.$\n\n- The series $$\n\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}\n$$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{1+3}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{0+3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{4}^{0}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges."] [:db/cas 17592186078349 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^-} \\dfrac {\\vert x^2 - 4 \\vert} {x - 2}$." "Determine $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 2^-} \\dfrac {\\vert x^2 - 4 \\vert} {x - 2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078358 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} }.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 4^+} \\dfrac {x - 2\\sqrt{x}} {x - 4} }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078359 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x +1} -1}  }.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\dfrac {3x} {\\sqrt{x +1} -1}  }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078371 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcee0bc0465b5cd26f982d11ea7de02c477c4215e4cbdb9b463241ed225b8850.png)" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0}f(x)\n$$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcee0bc0465b5cd26f982d11ea7de02c477c4215e4cbdb9b463241ed225b8850.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186078373 :question/problem "![](images/94/94ff7096b1befc0ce2538efc345ff3f090c66ee28f98bf62b6162cfed9226137.png)\n\nA dilation with center $O$ is defined by the function $(x,y)  \\mapsto \\left(\\dfrac53x, \\dfrac53y\\right).$  What is the image of the line shown above under the action of this transformation?" "![](images/94/94ff7096b1befc0ce2538efc345ff3f090c66ee28f98bf62b6162cfed9226137.png)\n\nA dilation with center $O$ is defined by the function $$\n\\displaystyle (x,y)  \\mapsto \\left(\\dfrac53x, \\dfrac53y\\right).\n$$  What is the image of the line shown above under the action of this transformation?"] [:db/cas 17592186078375 :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (1,5]$. Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 1}f(x)$ for the function.\n\n![](images/7c/7c596732a717c4aa97ce2079fab32f85779b31811d1de9872db491ce4cb775d3.png)" "The function $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (1,5]$. Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 1}f(x)\n$$ for the function.\n\n![](images/7c/7c596732a717c4aa97ce2079fab32f85779b31811d1de9872db491ce4cb775d3.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186078378 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} .$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{2\\sin{x}}{3x} .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078714 :question/problem "Express $S = \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{2}{5} + \\frac{3}{7} + ⋯ + \\frac{20}{41} + \\frac{21}{43}$ in sigma notation." "Express $$\n\\displaystyle S = \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{2}{5} + \\frac{3}{7} + ⋯ + \\frac{20}{41} + \\frac{21}{43}\n$$ in sigma notation."] [:db/cas 17592186078721 :question/problem "Find $f^{′}(1)$ if $f(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_{x}^{3x}e^{t^{2}}dt$." "Find $f^{′}(1)$ if $$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_{x}^{3x}e^{t^{2}}dt.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078725 :question/worked-solution "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the given triangle.\n\n![](images/38/38dcba98867c158c671b35ec033fa1ec55c8cedc67466ebe981de9fbbae314e9.png)\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{LM}$ opposite to the given angle $∠N$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{LN}$ adjacent$∠N.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠N) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =\\frac{8}{LN} \\\\ LN\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =8 \\\\ LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{(0.753\\,55…)} \\\\  & =10.616\\,35… \\\\  & ≈11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the given triangle.\n\n![](images/38/38dcba98867c158c671b35ec033fa1ec55c8cedc67466ebe981de9fbbae314e9.png)\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{LM}$ opposite to the given angle $∠N$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{LN}$ adjacent$∠N.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠N) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =\\frac{8}{LN} \\\\ LN\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =8 \\\\ LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{(0.753\\,55…)} \\\\  & =10.616\\,35… \\\\  & ≈11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."] [:db/cas 17592186078742 :question/problem "![](images/82/824f039d5f015a2cd2165cfd469c44e7f3e5a0cda6416964ec91d57de4ca06eb.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/82/824f039d5f015a2cd2165cfd469c44e7f3e5a0cda6416964ec91d57de4ca06eb.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078747 :question/problem "![](images/49/49ea038a841683b688cc5aeaa5c8c64c6028feb9c4362e1d919b31d7446ff91b.png)\n\nThe function $y = f(x)$ is defined on $x ∈ (- ∞,-1]∪[- \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}]∪[1, ∞)$. Find $lim_(x → - 1)f(x)$." "![](images/49/49ea038a841683b688cc5aeaa5c8c64c6028feb9c4362e1d919b31d7446ff91b.png)\n\nThe function $y = f(x)$ is defined on $$\n\\displaystyle x ∈ (- ∞,-1]∪[- \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}]∪[1, ∞).\n$$ Find $lim_(x → - 1)f(x)$."] [:db/cas 17592186078749 :question/problem "If $S(k) = ∑_(i = 1)^(k)(2i - 1)$, then $S(k + 1) =$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle S(k) = ∑_(i = 1)^(k)(2i - 1),\n$$ then $S(k + 1) =$"] [:db/cas 17592186078750 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{n}-1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{2}^{i}=2({{field-1}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{2}^{i}=2\\cdot ({{field-3}}).\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{2}^{i}=2\\cdot ({{field-4}}).\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{n}-1)$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{n}-1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{2}^{i}=2({{field-1}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{2}^{i}=2\\cdot ({{field-3}}).\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{2}^{i}=2\\cdot ({{field-4}}).\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{n}-1)\n$$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078750 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{n}-1)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{1}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{k}-1).\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{k+1}-1).\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{n}-1)$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{n}-1)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{1}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{k}-1).\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{k+1}-1).\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{2}^{i}=2({2}^{n}-1)\n$$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186078761 :question/problem "Prove that $∑_(i = 1)^(n)(2i - 1) = n^{2}$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n$.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n$2$ {{field-2}}\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n = 1$." "Prove that $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(2i - 1) = n^{2}\n$$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n$.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n$2$ {{field-2}}\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n = 1$."] [:db/cas 17592186078762 :question/problem "Prove that $∑_(i = 1)^(n)2^{i} = 2^{n + 1} - 2$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n$.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n$i$ {{field-2}}\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n = 1$." "Prove that $$\n\\displaystyle ∑_(i = 1)^(n)2^{i} = 2^{n + 1} - 2\n$$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n$.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n$i$ {{field-2}}\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n = 1$."] [:db/cas 17592186078763 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=2}^{n}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}n(n-1)(n+1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=2}^{2}i(i-1)=\\frac{1}{3}{{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We as